it's causing serious friction between  the united kingdom and the european  union as well as some serious supply  line issues but what is the northern  ireland protocol and why does it matter  the northern ireland protocol was  negotiated between the uk and the eu as  part of the brexit withdrawal agreement  it essentially governs trade across the  irish sea  as part of the protocol northern ireland  remains part of the uk's customs  territory but it also has to stick to  some eu rules so that goods can move  freely into the republic of ireland and  onto the rest of the eu  when ireland and northern ireland were  both in the eu they shared the same  trade rule so there was no need for any  sort of rigorous checks on goods  traveling between them but then brexit  happens and a new agreement was needed  and suddenly it became the main log jam  during the brexit negotiations  all sides agreed that protecting the  good friday agreement was an absolute  priority that meant keeping the land  border between northern ireland and the  republic of ireland open and avoiding a  hard border  the issue here is that the republic of  ireland and northern ireland did once  have a hard border which came about as a  result of the troubles which saw  communities separated and violence  between opposing groups for almost 30  years  remember boris johnson describing the  protocol as a good arrangement and  promising there be no checks on trade  between britain and northern ireland  well that hasn't happened instead  there's been delays with goods like  medicines arriving from britain and  problems with pets on the movement of  live animals on seeds and plants as well  as occasionally some pretty sparse  supermarket shelves  the protocol is not working  it's completely lost consent in one  community in northern ireland it's not  doing the thing it was set up to do now  the europeans have proposed a raft of  measures to ease those issues cutting  checks they say by up to 80  but bars johnson and his team say the eu  has to totally amend the protocol and  they want to scrap the role of the  european court of justice as a sort of  referee for disputes we need to be able  to just pick up the phone order the  produce in the uk and let it arrive here  the way it used to all of this means  that britain is now threatening to  trigger article 16. that's basically  something to be used in the worst case  scenario where the protocol is deemed to  be causing economic societal or  environmental difficulties liable to  persist or to the diversion of trade it  means that either the eu or the uk can  then suspend parts of the withdrawal  agreement but it is being seen very much  as an option of last resort and it's not  on the table for the moment  so can either side throw a lifeline to  the beleaguered protocol it's the time  once again for intense talks and  negotiations between britain and the eu  to see if they can tweak it so it works  for both sides  if things go badly that could lead to  the uk triggering article 16 but that  could also lead to retaliation by the eu  potentially including new tariffs on  british imports and a potential trade  war  realistically both sides will be doing  everything they can to try and avoid  that nightmare scenario after all we've  already had a sausage war out of the  protocol let's hope it's not a taste of  things to come
 

